  "body": "https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#algorithm-control-flow mentions the ability to \"catch\", but it isn't clear how to use it. I guess you can only catch within a single step like:\r\n\r\n1. Let |whatever| be the result of [=doing something=] with |aThing|. If it throws, catch the error and let |whatever| be null.\r\n\r\nDo we need \"try\"?\r\n\r\n1. Let |whatever| be null.\r\n1. Try:\r\n    1. Set |whatever| to the result of [=doing something=] with |aThing|.\r\n1. Catch any error.\r\n\r\nThis would be more useful if the catch involves multiple steps.\r\n\r\n1. Let |whatever| be null.\r\n1. Try:\r\n    1. Set |whatever| to the result of [=doing something=] with |aThing|.\r\n1. Catch any error:\r\n    1. …\r\n    1. …",
